          "text": "{\"target_identification\":{\"speaker\":\"The rose\",\"addressee\":\"The little prince\",\"anchor_quote\":\"je t'aime\"},\"target_translation\":\"I love you.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"The focal expression directly avows the rose's loving emotional state. The following admission that the little prince did not know it because of her fault reinforces its force as a disclosure of a previously concealed feeling.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"je t'aime\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Tu n'en a rien su, par ma faute.\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.9,\"reason\":\"The declaration does not create, renew, or invoke an obligation in this farewell. The rose instead releases the little prince and urges him to leave and be happy. Those directives are separate acts rather than terms of a commitment enacted by “je t'aime.”\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Tâche d'être heureux...Laisse ce globe tranquille. Je n'en veux plus.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Tu as décidé de partir. Va-t'en.\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.86,\"reason\":\"Although the scene is emotionally charged and the rose conceals tears, the focal words are presented as a calm, intelligible admission rather than a reflex-like affective discharge.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Il ne comprenait pas cette douceur calme.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Car elle ne voulait pas qu'il la vît pleurer.\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.91,\"reason\":\"No core force beyond the avowal is needed to characterize the focal expression. Its revelatory and explanatory role is adequately covered by T, while the apology and farewell directives are distinct speech acts in the surrounding turn.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Mais oui, je t'aime, lui dit la fleur. Tu n'en a rien su, par ma faute.\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"During their farewell, as the little prince prepares to depart, the rose apologizes and calmly reveals that she loves him and had failed to let him know. She then relinquishes his protection and tells him to leave and seek happiness.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"The focal declaration contains no supported undertaking or obligation. The rose's surrounding instructions release the little prince rather than bind either party. No explicit verbal uptake of the love declaration is supplied; his subsequent objections concern leaving her exposed to wind and animals.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The supplied passage is recognisable as Chapter IX of Antoine de Saint-Exupéry's Le Petit Prince.\",\"limitations\":\"The excerpt strongly supports an avowal and supplies behavioral evidence of affection, but it does not provide an explicit response accepting or rejecting the declaration, nor does it establish broader relationship terms beyond this farewell scene.\"}"
        }
